-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Game Over
  Publisher   Imagine / Dinamic, 1987
  Version     1.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Game Over (1987)(Imagine)(128K)[re-release].z80
  MD5         e1c2ff3cfbd4f6f5a84034d57b666acf

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures are drawn on the Next's own graphics layers, so they keep their
  own colours and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is
  unchanged.

  Colours     Default (the game's own) plus: Enhanced, chosen on the menu, off
              by default.
  Enhanced    The Spec256 conversion's colours on every figure and all the
              scenery: the soldier in blue armour, the hover-riders, the iron
              arches and the jungle cliffs, the grey block frame and the
              panel's text and icons. Figures keep a black mask edge against
              the scenery. Backdrop adds world 1's red planet and starfield
              and world 2's stars; it comes on with Enhanced, and the Backdrop
              off line takes it away. Source: Enhanced colours: David Goti
              (Spec256).

  Pace        original (-1.03% measured in JNext 1.1.104, against the original
              .z80 on a 128K machine, game state in lockstep)

  Changes     1.0: figures in their own colours on a double-buffered Layer 2,
              in the game's own plot order, over the original bitmap; scenery
              in its own attributes; the barrel's own cell colours; both
              worlds; the plot, restore and copy run whole at 28 MHz in pace
              windows; a loading screen from the same re-release's tape;
              Sinclair's 48K ROM as the whole alt ROM (NextZXOS 26.11 safe);
              the game's speed restored after every hook
              1.1: Enhanced colours as a menu option, off by default, from the
              Spec256 conversion: every figure, all the scenery, the frame,
              panel and text on a full-screen Layer 2; world 1's planet and
              starfield and world 2's stars as a backdrop picture that comes
              on with Enhanced and has its own Backdrop off line; both fit a
              1MB Next. World 2's pace in the default look: a plot's window
              now comes from what it draws, so scene changes and quiet scenes
              keep the original's time

  Note        Keys: on the game's menu 1 keyboard (it asks for each key), 2
              joystick, 3 play, 4 the other world; world 2 asks for the access
              code the game gives at the end of world 1. The original look:
              figures keep their own colours and the scenery keeps its own,
              each figure's ink drawn on Layer 2 in the game's own order over
              the original bitmap. The 128K release, both worlds, with its AY
              music. Fits a 1MB Next, Enhanced included. Known issues: in
              JNext whole runs are 0.4 to 1.1 percent faster than the original
              in the default look and 1.3 to 2.2 percent in Enhanced; the
              slowest 20-update stretch is +4.9 percent, in world 2's default
              look, and +1.9 percent in Enhanced; a scene change takes up to 6
              percent longer (about 2 frames); an occasional interrupt can be
              lost while the game's own sound code has interrupts off.
              Missing: the earth-shake moves nothing in Enhanced; no cheats
              (the archive's POKEs do not match this release); natural play to
              the end; hardware untested.

